WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:03.030
- [Narrator] After more than a year

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.870
of living with COVID-19
safety precautions,

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.350
quarantines and social isolation,

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:11.933
life is starting to return
back to pre-COVID normal.

00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:13.070
(dog barking)

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:14.480
And that's forcing our brains

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.560
to rethink how we behave socially.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.800
- Now we have to make individual decisions

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:19.810
about what are environments

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:20.870
in which we feel comfortable

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.750
and what are environments where
we don't feel comfortable?

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.430
- [Narrator] Should I eat indoors,

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:27.603
go to that concert, wear my mask?

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:31.150
A new mental toll is emerging.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:34.750
Many people are reevaluating
the habits they formed

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.430
during the pandemic

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:38.420
and reassessing how safe it is

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:41.360
to resume once-normal activities.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.410
But why do some of us find it difficult

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.860
to make these choices?

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.230
It has to do with how our
brains process information.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.780
Let's take a look at what happens

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.110
when you're finally reunited with friends

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.412
and you're faced with a choice.

00:00:54.412 --> 00:00:55.780
(people murmuring)

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.290
Mia is in her 40s,
healthy, fully vaccinated

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:02.330
and is attending a
backyard barbecue party.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:03.620
But it starts to rain

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.423
and the party moves indoors.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.270
Though CDC guidelines say the risk

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:10.450
of infection is minimal
for people like Mia

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.140
who are fully vaccinated,
she has to decide

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.510
whether she feels
comfortable going indoors

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.530
with a big group of people.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.110
- When we're making those
decisions in our brains,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.600
what we're really doing is
weighing three sets of factors.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.790
The first factor's almost
the easiest to understand

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.010
and that's what risks am I willing to take

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.680
when I understand the risks completely?

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.640
- [Narrator] Making a
choice can happen quickly

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.660
as several parts of
the brain work together

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:33.890
to come to a decision.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.760
When people begin to move indoors,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.020
Mia faces a known risk:

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.840
the chance she could
get exposed to COVID-19.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:44.200
Her prefrontal cortex
starts to assess that risk

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.710
and the possible outcomes of her actions.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.840
The second is uncertainty aversion.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.290
Things that are ambiguous and obscure.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:54.300
That assessment happens
in part in the amygdala,

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.710
which is associated with processing fear,

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:58.550
distress or anxiety.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.180
The more ambiguous a situation is,

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.950
the more likely someone is to be cautious.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.570
For Mia, the ambiguous risk is tied

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.240
to how much she doesn't know.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.930
Is everyone vaccinated?

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.180
How high are infection rates in town.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.530
Is there enough ventilation in the house?

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.610
If she has a good handle
on all this information,

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.930
her ambiguous risk is lower

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.070
and she may opt in favor of the reward

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:24.050
to go inside and continue spending time

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.410
with her friends.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.840
The third factor is the habit system.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.380
That's located in the dorsal striatum,

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.300
near the amygdala.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.770
- When we come into an environment

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:36.790
that we've been to a million
times before the pandemic

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.610
and we have some set of behaviors

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:40.810
that we've learned to produce
in those environments,

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.460
like walking up to
someone and hugging them,

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.400
these habits are gonna also
influence our behavior.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.810
- [Narrator] Prior to the pandemic,

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:51.090
Mia used to hang out at her
friends' homes regularly.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.520
But COVID changed a lot of her habits.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.563
She only saw people outdoors.

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:59.610
- In each of these brain areas,

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.740
there are millions of brain cells.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.670
And those brain cells
generate action potentials,

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.800
these little electrochemical impulses

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.760
with which they send
information from one part

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.300
of the brain to another.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.220
- [Narrator] So as Mia weighs her choice,

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.930
the brain is calculating
the risk and reward

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.243
of going inside versus going home.

00:03:20.352 --> 00:03:22.370
In the final stage of decision making,

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.830
information from multiple
parts of the brain

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:27.780
is integrated to make a final choice.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.080
This includes the prefrontal cortex

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.870
at the very front of the brain

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:33.600
and the ventral striatum,

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.760
a neighboring region to the
dorsal striatum and amygdala.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:40.650
- So there is where we
find groups of nerve cells

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.650
that represent each of the options

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:45.760
and have aggregated together
all of the different factors

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.190
that are gonna influence how much you like

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.440
or don't like doing that.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.320
And then in these areas,
in areas closely associated

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.870
with them, that's where we
expect to see this competition

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:59.860
where the group of cells
that is most active,

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.500
the ones that are encoding
the more desirable option

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.420
with greater electrochemical activity

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.030
will sort of take control

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:07.270
and lead us to the behavior

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:08.570
that we've chosen to make.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:10.850
- [Narrator] But we're all different.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.820
Our personalities, age and
life experiences influence

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.840
all three systems uniquely.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.020
So what Mia decides could be different

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.700
from her friend Mark.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.740
- Some of us are more
concerned about ambiguity.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.030
Maybe because of prior life trauma.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.310
Some of us are more concerned about risk

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:29.960
and some of us are more driven by habits.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.610
So there're individual differences

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.080
in the way we weigh each of
these three sets of factors.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.990
And then, of course, we
all have different inputs

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:38.422
from the world around us.

00:04:38.422 --> 00:04:39.660
- [Narrator] Another factor

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.170
that could influence our decisions

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:43.500
over the next few months may be related

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.560
to trauma from the pandemic.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.480
Dr. Glimcher's actively studying

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:50.510
how the events of the last
year can affect our choices.

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:53.580
He says there's still
a lot of unknowns but.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.930
- We do know that while trauma
changes the way people decide

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:59.210
and changes the way people behave,

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.760
it makes them more ambiguity
averse, for example,

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.120
it doesn't completely disorder the brain.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.930
The basic rules and structures

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.090
for making decisions remain the same.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:11.090
It's the weightings that are different.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:13.330
- [Narrator] So for Mark,
who lost his grandfather

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.250
to COVID-19, the decision
of whether to stay

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.770
or leave the party could
be weighted differently due

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:20.340
to that trauma.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.151
As the pandemic eases in
many parts of the world,

00:05:23.151 --> 00:05:26.530
it's unclear how trauma
and fear associated

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:27.730
with the past year

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:30.330
will continue to impact people's lives.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.060
But the brain is flexible.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.470
It constantly learns from experience

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.060
and as we're testing our
boundaries of what feels safe

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:39.350
and what doesn't, we're learning to adjust

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:42.160
to all situations that now feel new.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.210
It's that learning that will
ultimately help us get back

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:46.373
to a new normal.

